"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Rector_of_Justin", "title": "The Rector of Justin - Wikipedia", "content": "# *The Rector of Justin*. 
***The Rector of Justin*** is a 1964 psychological fiction novel by Louis Auchincloss about the headmaster (or \"rector \"Rector 
(ecclesiastical)\")\") of a socially exclusive American boarding school. Through the narrators' disagreements, the novel gradually unveils 
that White Anglo-Saxon Protestant society—of which Prescott is a reluctant mascot—has lost its innocence and abandoned its Christian values. 
The novel was a commercial hit and was warmly received by most contemporary critics. It was a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction and
the National Book Award for Fiction, losing to Shirley Ann Grau's *The Keeper of the House* and Saul Bellow's *Herzog \"Herzog (novel)\")*, 
respectively. The story is told through six narrators: frame narrator Brian Aspinwall, a teacher at Massachusetts Episcopal \"Episcopal 
Church (United States)\") boarding school Justin Martyr (\"Justin\" for short; named for the Christian figure), who is asked to write a 
biography of its founder Francis Prescott; and writings and interviews from five people who knew him.", "score": 0.7192461, "raw_content": 
"![](/static/images/icons/enwiki-25.svg)\n![Wikipedia](/static/images/mobile/copyright/wikipedia-wordmark-en-25.svg)\n![The Free 
Encyclopedia](/static/images/mobile/copyright/wikipedia-tagline-en-25.svg)\n\n## Contents\n\n# *The Rector of Justin*\n\n![This is a good 
article. Click here for more 
information.](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/94/Symbol_support_vote.svg/20px-Symbol_support_vote.svg.png)\n\nThe Rector of 
Justin\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Photo of the original front cover](/wiki/File:The_Rector_of_Justin_1964_hardcover_dust_jacket.png 
\"Photo of the original front cover\") Cover of the 1964 first edition | |\n| Author | [Louis Auchincloss](/wiki/Louis_Auchincloss \"Louis 
Auchincloss\") |\n| Language | English |\n| Genre | [Psychological fiction](/wiki/Psychological_fiction \"Psychological fiction\") |\n| 
Publisher | [Houghton Mifflin](/wiki/Houghton_Mifflin \"Houghton Mifflin\") |\n| Publication date | 1964 |\n| [ISBN](/wiki/ISBN_(identifier) 
\"ISBN (identifier)\") | [978-0-618-22489-0](/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-618-22489-0 \"Special:BookSources/978-0-618-22489-0\") 
|\n\n![Photo of the original front 
cover](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/8a/The_Rector_of_Justin_1964_hardcover_dust_jacket.png/250px-The_Rector_of_Justin_1964_har
dcover_dust_jacket.png)\n\n***The Rector of Justin*** is a 1964 [psychological fiction](/wiki/Psychological_fiction \"Psychological 
fiction\") novel by [Louis Auchincloss](/wiki/Louis_Auchincloss \"Louis Auchincloss\") about the headmaster (or 
\"[rector](/wiki/Rector_(ecclesiastical) \"Rector (ecclesiastical)\")\") of a socially exclusive American [boarding 
school](/wiki/Boarding_school \"Boarding school\"). Over the decades, various narrators provide contrasting perspectives on rector Francis 
Prescott's charismatic personality and autocratic leadership style. Through the narrators' disagreements, the novel gradually unveils that 
[White Anglo-Saxon Protestant](/wiki/White_Anglo-Saxon_Protestants \"White Anglo-Saxon Protestants\") society—of which Prescott is a 
reluctant mascot—has lost its innocence and abandoned its Christian values.\n\nThe novel was a commercial hit and was warmly received by most
contemporary critics. It was a finalist for the [Pulitzer Prize for Fiction](/wiki/Pulitzer_Prize_for_Fiction \"Pulitzer Prize for Fiction\")
and the [National Book Award for Fiction](/wiki/National_Book_Award_for_Fiction \"National Book Award for Fiction\"), losing to [Shirley Ann 
Grau](/wiki/Shirley_Ann_Grau \"Shirley Ann Grau\")'s *[The Keeper of the House](/wiki/The_Keepers_of_the_House \"The Keepers of the House\")*
