Starters | EBSCO Research", "content": "Philip Barry was an influential American playwright known for his comedic dramas that often explored 
themes of high society, romance, and moral dilemmas.", "score": 0.3534064, "raw_content": "RESEARCH STARTER\n\n# Philip Barry\n\nPhilip Barry
was an influential American playwright known for his comedic dramas that often explored themes of high society, romance, and moral dilemmas. 
Born in 1896 into an Irish Catholic middle-class family in Rochester, New York, Barry demonstrated a passion for literature and theater early
on, which led him to Yale University. After completing his degree, he honed his craft at Harvard's Workshop 47 under the mentorship of George
Pierce Baker, where he developed into a successful playwright.\n\nHis notable works include \"You and I,\" \"The Youngest,\" \"Holiday,\" and
\"The Philadelphia Story,\" with the latter two adapted into successful films featuring Katharine Hepburn. Barry's plays are characterized by
clever dialogue and the portrayal of affluent characters navigating personal conflicts, often culminating in resolutions reflective of 
intelligence and common sense. Beyond comedy, he also ventured into darker themes in works like \"Hotel Universe\" and \"Here Come the 
Clowns,\" showcasing a range of human experience. Despite a decline in performance frequency by the late twentieth century, Barry's legacy as
a master of high comedy endures, highlighting the complexities and triumphs of society during the post-World War I era. He passed away in 
1949, leaving behind a diverse body of work that merits further exploration.\n\nAuthored By: Fiorelli, Edward 1 of 2\n\nPublished In: 2023 2 
of 2\n\n## On This Page\n\n## Full Article\n\n### Philip Barry\n\nAmerican playwright and author\n\n### Biography\n\nLike a character from 
his own sparkling plays, Philip Barry had the good fortune to be handsome, clever, and rich. Born into an Irish Catholic middle-class family 
(his dying father left him unprovided for in his will), he was fortunate in his opportunities for education, marriage, and the fulfillment of
his talent. After finishing public high school in Rochester, New York, Barry was accepted into Yale University in 1913, when he was 
seventeen. From his freshman year he showed a keen interest in literature; he read avidly and wrote poems and stories for the Yale literary 
magazine. He spent a year in London as a code clerk for the American embassy during World War I and afterward returned to the United States, 
receiving a degree from Yale in 1919.\n\nBarry’s interest in the theater, evident even in his early teens, now ripened into an ambition that 
would make him one of the most successful playwrights of his time. He enrolled in George Pierce Baker’s Workshop 47 drama course at Harvard 
University in 1919 and earned Baker’s respect and friendship. Baker perceived the authenticity of Barry’s talent and gave him the 
encouragement and knowledge that made the workshop indispensable not only to Barry but also to a generation of American dramatists, including
Eugene O’Neill.\n\nBarry’s first full-length play, *A Punch for Judy*, was completed at the workshop and produced in 1921. By this time, he 
had met Ellen Semple, a wealthy debutante and an artist, and the two were married in July, 1922. For a wedding present the couple was given a
house in Cannes, on the French Riviera, and for the rest of his life Barry moved between Broadway and Cannes as his career blossomed. Their 
son Philip Jr. went on to become a famed producer of stage, cinema, and broadcast productions.\n\nHis first major success was the comedy *You
and I*, which ran on Broadway for 170 performances and received the Herndon Prize. The play was characteristic of the well-made comedy of 
manners that became Barry’s hallmark: clever, witty dialogue among charming men and women of high society, a romance with its conflicts, and 
an artfully satisfying resolution.\n\nHis next play, *The Youngest*, about the younger generation’s conflict with the conventions of family 
life, established Barry as the foremost American dramatist of the comedy of manners. Yet it was *Paris Bound*, *Holiday*, and, especially, 
*The Philadelphia Story* that earned for him a significant place in American dram…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
