[AIMessage → tool] create_analysis({'text': 'Synthesis for article drafting: Evidence consistently identifies "The Rector of Justin" as a 
1964 novel by Louis Auchincloss, not a play by Philip Barry. The book is set at Justin Martyr, an elite New England boys\' boarding school 
founded and led by aging headmaster (rector) Francis Prescott. The story is framed through Hugh Carver, a young teacher and clergyman who is 
writing Prescott\'s biography, and through additional narrative voices that complicate Prescott\'s public image.[1][2][3] Plot and thematic 
discussions describe the novel as following Prescott late in his career as he faces retirement, institutional politics, and revelations about
his past, including tensions within his family and questions about his ambition and use of power in building the school.[2][4] Critics and 
teachers note that the novel explores themes of moral authority, religious conviction, the ethics of educational leadership, social class, 
and the gap between public reputation and private character, and it is often grouped with American "campus" or "school" novels.[2][3][4] 
Publication records from 1964 and later editions confirm its status as a work of fiction and one of Auchincloss\'s better-known novels, which
has continued to appear on reading lists and in scholarship on American education and campus fiction.[1][3] This synthesis can ground the 
