[tools:7366ceb0-5664-96d7-c639-8930aeb27cb1] [submit_final_report result] {"accepted": true, "revision_requested": true, "severity": "soft", 
"report": "", "salvaged_report": "# The Rector of Justin\n\n## Lead\n\nThe Rector of Justin is a 1964 novel by American writer Louis 
Auchincloss, set at an elite boys\u0019 boarding school in New England and centered on the career and late-life self-examination of its 
founding headmaster and rector. Classified by library and catalog records as a work of fiction, the book employs multiple narrative 
perspectives to examine the tension between the rector\u0019s public image as a moral and institutional leader and his more conflicted 
private self. Contemporary and later criticism has treated The Rector of Justin as one of Auchincloss\u0019s most ambitious works, and it 
achieved early commercial prominence by appearing on The New York Times best seller list and in national magazine coverage shortly after 
publication. Research into Philip Barry\u0019s oeuvre and theatrical catalogs has found no evidence of a play titled The Rector of Justin by 
Barry or any other dramatist, indicating that the title is associated in published sources only with Auchincloss\u0019s novel. 
[15][16][18][31][8][30][3][29][34][21][22][23][24][25][38]\n\n## Background and authorship\n\nLibrary of Congress catalog and biographical 
notes identify The Rector of Justin as a novel by Louis Auchincloss, with subject headings placing it among works of fiction about 
preparatory schools, school principals, New England, and boys. A related Library of Congress contributor biography, prepared for 
Auchincloss\u0019s novel The Education of Oscar Fairfax, states that he \"has written more than sixty books\" and explicitly lists The Rector
of Justin among his novels. WorldCat\u0019s union catalog record for The rector of Justin (OCLC 5751292) similarly describes the work as a 
novel by Auchincloss, clustering multiple printings and editions under the same author and title. [16][17][18][15]\n\nPublisher-oriented 
materials and institutional summaries of Auchincloss\u0019s career describe him as a Wall Street lawyer and prolific novelist whose first 
novel, The Indifferent Children, appeared in 1947, and they list The Rector of Justin among his notable later works. An American Library in 
Paris newsletter notes that Auchincloss had \"a successful career as a Wall Street lawyer\" while producing numerous books, including The 
Rector of Justin, reinforcing its status as part of his ongoing exploration of upper-class American institutions. [32][19]\n\nResearch into 
Philip Barry\u0019s bibliography and archival holdings shows no record of a play titled The Rector of Justin in his body of work. 
Biographical entries and reference summaries of Barry\u0019s career list plays such as Paris Bound, Holiday, and The Philadelphia Story, but 
do not mention a work by this title. Catalogs of his authored material in Open Library and archival finding aids at Yale University and 
Georgetown University similarly enumerate his dramatic works without any entry for The Rector of Justin. A monograph on Barry\u0019s plays, 
Shadowed Cocktails: The Plays of Philip Barry from Paris Bound to The Philadelphia Story, focuses on his known stage works and does not 
include The Rector of Justin among them. [20][21][27][23][24][25]\n\nBarry\u0019s filmography and screenwriting credits, as listed in 
databases such as IMDb and theater-industry records like the Playbill Vault, similarly confine his work to a recognizable list of plays and 
adaptations without reference to The Rector of Justin. Taken together, these bibliographic, archival, and industry sources support the 
conclusion that The Rector of Justin is not a play by Philip Barry and that the title in published records refers exclusively to 
Auchincloss\u0019s novel. [26][38]\n\n## Plot\n\nThe Rector of Justin is set at a prestigious private boys\u0019 school in New England and 
centers on the institution\u0019s aging founder and rector, Frank Prescott, whose life and character are examined through the recollections 
and perspectives of people around him. Commentary on the novel describes Prescott as a towering public figure at the school whose reputation 
and myth have been built over decades of leadership, while the narrative gradually reveals his more complex inner life and the frailties 
behind his authoritative image. [2][16][30][8]\n\nPlot-focused discussion from a literary blog summarizes an important strand of the story 
through the character Brian Aspinwall, who becomes a teacher at age twenty-seven at the prestigious New England school after being suddenly 
recruited. Aspinwall\u0019s arrival provides a younger perspective on the institution and its rector, enabling readers to see the 
school\u0019s traditions and Prescott\u0019s leadership through the eyes of a newcomer whose admiration and doubts evolve over time. 
[36][30]\n\nCritical summaries emphasize that the narrative unfolds through a series of viewpoints, each adding layers to the portrayal of 
Prescott and the school; this structure underscores how different observers construct and deconstruct his reputation. As these voices revisit
key episodes in the school\u0019s history and in Prescott\u0019s personal life, including his family relationships and religious commitments,
the plot moves toward a portrait of an institutional leader confronting his legacy and the divergence between his ideals and their 
realization. [8][30][29]\n\n## Characters\n\nFrank Prescott, the founding headmaster and rector of the fictional New England school, is the 
central figure of the novel, depicted as a charismatic yet increasingly controversial leader whose public image as a moral and spiritual 
guide is gradually questioned. Library of Congress subject headings categorize the book under \"School principals \u0014 Fiction\" and 
\"Preparatory schools \u0014 Fiction\", reflecting Prescott\u0019s dual roles as institutional head and teacher within a boarding school 
environment. [2][16][29][30]\n\nBrian Aspinwall, introduced in one plot summary as a young instructor who joins the school at twenty-seven 
after being recruited, serves as a key observer of Prescott and the institution. Through Aspinwall\u0019s impressions and changing attitudes,
the novel juxtaposes the rector\u0019s established authority with the perspectives of a newer generation of faculty and students encountering
the school\u0019s traditions under changing cultural conditions. [36][30]\n\nCritical and reference discussions indicate that the book 
features a range of other characters connected to the school\u0019s community, including colleagues, students, and family members, who 
contribute additional narrative viewpoints on Prescott and the institution. These figures help articulate the tensions between reverence and 
skepticism toward the rector, illustrating how his leadership shapes and is challenged by the diverse people living and working within the 
boarding school. [2][6][16][8][30]\n\n## Themes\n\nReview and critical commentary highlight a central thematic concern with the contrast 
between public image and private self, especially as it applies to a revered institutional leader. One contemporary note characterizes the 
book as about \"finding the man behind the fa\u0000e7ade, the individual back of the image\", suggesting that Auchincloss uses the 
multiple-narrator structure to explore how Prescott\u0019s reputation as rector conceals a more complicated personality and history. 
[8][30]\n\nCritics also identify themes of vocation and institutional mission, particularly the conflict between an educator\u0019s 
dedication to an elite boarding school and his responsibilities to family and faith. Commentators note that the novel portrays late-life 
reflection, moral failure, and the erosion or compromise of religious convictions within structures of authority, presenting Prescott as 
someone whose ideals are tested by ambition, power, and the practical realities of running a school. [8][30]\n\nThe American elite boarding 
school setting allows Auchincloss to examine privilege, social reproduction, and institutional culture.\u0000a0One magazine article connects 
The Rector of Justin with Massachusetts\u0019 Groton School and its founder Endicott Peabody, describing Auchincloss as \"no better 
chronicler\" of that environment and indicating that the fictional school and rector draw on this tradition of educating the country\u0019s 
social and political elites. Commentators argue that the novel depicts the school both as a training ground for the privileged and as a stage
for moral compromise and institutional power struggles. [29][8][31]\n\nMore broadly, the book has been discussed as part of 
Auchincloss\u0019s recurring interest in upper-class American life and institutional settings such as schools and law firms, employing a 
dryly ironic style that some critics describe as Jamesian. In this context, The Rector of Justin is seen as examining leadership, ambition, 
failure, and self-mythologizing within a particular social milieu rather than presenting a simple moral allegory. [4][32][8][30]\n\n## 
Composition and publication\n\nBookseller catalog information records The Rector of Justin in a sequence of editions beginning with a 1964 
volume and followed by later printings issued by publishers such as Modern Library and Franklin Library, with dates including circa 1967, 
1977, and 2001. Library and catalog sources consistently describe the book as \"originally published in 1964\", confirming that year as the 
date of first appearance. [1][3][31][32]\n\nLibrary of Congress and WorldCat records indicate that The Rector of Justin has been reissued in 
multiple formats, including standard trade editions and later printings that keep the novel available in library collections. Subject 
headings in the Library of Congress catalog classify the work under fiction about preparatory schools and school principals in New England, 
while WorldCat\u0019s work-level entry groups numerous editions of the novel under a single bibliographic record. [15][16][17]\n\nA Modern 
Library catalog page describes The Rector of Justin as \"widely considered to be [Louis Auchincloss\u0019s] most ambitious novel and his 
greatest achievement\" and identifies it as part of the publisher\u0019s series of classic novels. The same page notes that the book is a 
novel, not a play, and situates it among Modern Library\u0019s reprints of significant twentieth-century fiction. [31]\n\n## Reception and 
legacy\n\nA contemporary bibliographic source notes that The Rector of Justin, published in 1964, appeared on The New York Times best seller 
list, indicating substantial early readership and commercial success. A 1960s critical survey of modern American literature refers to the 
novel as \"a tale of considerable distinction\" and comments on Auchincloss\u0019s \"dryishly ironic, Jamesian\" manner in works of this 
period, placing The Rector of Justin among his notable achievements. [3][4]\n\nTIME magazine coverage from 1964 links The Rector of Justin to
Massachusetts\u0019 Groton School and its founder Endicott Peabody, praising Auchincloss as \"no better chronicler\" of the school and its 
\"wise, eccentric\" leader. Another TIME listing of noteworthy books ranks The Rector of Justin fifth, further underscoring its visibility in
national media during the year of publication. [29][34]\n\nIn the early twenty-first century, institutional and personal reading lists have 
kept the book in circulation among dedicated readers. A newsletter from the American Library in Paris and a suggested reading list for a 
fiction workshop both include The Rector of Justin alongside other notable novels, indicating its continued appeal for discussion and study. 
Public library book clubs at institutions such as Falmouth Public Library and Plymouth District Library have selected the novel for group 
reading, demonstrating ongoing community interest in its portrayal of school culture and leadership. [32][13][33][37]\n\nMore recent 
commentary includes a 2017\u00142018 \"Beach Books\" guide that briefly introduces The Rector of Justin as a novel narrated from different 
points of view about Frank Prescott, founding headmaster of a fictional New England school, and a 2025 article in Catholic World Report that 
lists the book among the best read by the author that year, emphasizing its exploration of character, faith, and institutional life. These 
references, along with its inclusion in reference works like Masterplots and the Cyclopedia of Literary Characters, suggest that The Rector 
of Justin remains recognized as a significant academic novel about education and leadership. [2][30][5][6][34]\n\n## Adaptations\n\nAvailable
bibliographic, archival, and catalog sources attribute The Rector of Justin solely to Louis Auchincloss\u0019s 1964 novel and do not list any
film, television, or stage adaptations under this title. Reference works on Philip Barry\u0019s plays and theater-industry databases 
documenting his productions similarly include no work titled The Rector of Justin, reinforcing the conclusion that there is no documented 
Barry play or other dramatic adaptation bearing this title. [15][16][17][20][23][24][26]\n\n## References\n\n- Quill & Brush signed books 
